ONEDRAFT
CAD-AI — Aria Powered
Computational Project Model & Geometry Engine
Version 1.0
Status: Implementation Specification
Database: PostgreSQL
API: REST / JSON
API Version: /api/v1
Identifiers: UUID
Primary Model: Versioned Computational Project Model
AI Layer: Aria Powered
Geometry Layer: Parametric / Constraint-Aware
Coordinate System: Project-local Cartesian coordinates with explicit units
Primary Output: Validated computational building model

1. ENGINEERING OBJECTIVE
The Computational Project Model is the authoritative geometric representation of the building being designed.
OneDraft shall not treat drawings as the primary representation of architectural information.
The hierarchy is:
USER SPECIFICATION
        ↓
PROJECT MODEL
        ↓
PARAMETERS
        ↓
CONSTRAINTS
        ↓
GEOMETRY
        ↓
VIEWS
        ↓
DRAWINGS
        ↓
DOCUMENT PACKAGE
Aria operates on the Project Model.
The Geometry Engine resolves the spatial consequences of model changes.
The Documentation Engine derives architectural drawings from the resulting model.

2. COMPUTATIONAL MODEL PRINCIPLE
Every architectural object shall have:
identity
type
parameters
relationships
constraints
geometry
provenance
version
status
An object is therefore more than its graphical representation.
For example, a wall is not merely a line.
A wall contains:
wall identity
host level
location
length
height
thickness
orientation
assembly
openings
connections
constraints
fire properties
acoustic properties
thermal properties
geometry
The drawing representation is derived from these properties.

3. MODEL HIERARCHY
The initial computational hierarchy shall be:
PROJECT
 └── SITE
      └── BUILDING
           ├── GRID
           ├── LEVEL
           │    ├── SPACE
           │    └── ELEMENT
           │         ├── WALL
           │         ├── DOOR
           │         ├── WINDOW
           │         ├── COLUMN
           │         ├── BEAM
           │         ├── SLAB
           │         ├── ROOF
           │         └── OTHER
           │
           └── BUILDING SYSTEMS
The hierarchy must remain extensible.

4. MODEL OBJECT IDENTITY
Every computational object receives a UUID.
Example:
element_id
The UUID remains stable across ordinary geometric modifications.
Changing a wall's length does not create a new wall identity.
A new identity is created only when the semantic object itself is replaced or split according to defined model rules.

5. MODEL OBJECT STATE
Each object shall maintain:
ACTIVE
PROPOSED
SUPPRESSED
INVALID
DELETED
The state is distinct from geometric validity.
For example:
ACTIVE + VALID
ACTIVE + INVALID
PROPOSED + VALID
PROPOSED + INVALID
This distinction allows Aria to reason about proposed design changes without immediately committing them to the active model.

6. MODEL VERSION
Every coherent project state is represented by a Model Version.
Example:
Model Version 1
Model Version 2
Model Version 3
A model version identifies the complete semantic state against which geometry and documentation are evaluated.
The model version must be immutable once finalized.

7. REVISION
A Revision represents a meaningful project change.
Example:
Revision 1
    Initial project

Revision 2
    Revised rear wall

Revision 3
    Revised window configuration
A revision references a model version.
Therefore:
Revision
    ↓
Model Version
    ↓
Project Objects

8. COORDINATE SYSTEM
The geometry engine shall use a project-local Cartesian coordinate system.
Initial convention:
X = East / West
Y = North / South
Z = Vertical
The project coordinate origin shall be explicitly stored.
Example:
Origin = (0,0,0)
The project shall also maintain:
north_angle
elevation_reference
unit_system
coordinate_system

9. UNITS
All internal geometric calculations shall use a single canonical unit.
The initial canonical unit shall be:
millimetres
External units may include:
mm
cm
m
in
ft
Conversion shall occur at the API boundary or presentation layer.
The geometry engine shall not perform mixed-unit calculations.

